Below is a self-contained Rust function that computes the rank of each element in a 1-D array without sorting the list more than once. It uses a single sort of the index permutation and assigns ranks so equal values receive the same rank equal to the first position of that value in the sorted order (0-based). The function is written using the widely used ndarray crate; if you really need to use a different "narray" crate, the same algorithm applies and you can swap imports/types as appropriate.

Behavior summary:
- Ranks are 0-based.
- Equal elements receive the same rank (the minimal position among the equal elements in the sorted order).
- Only one sort is performed (on indices).

Code:

```rust
use ndarray::Array1;
use std::cmp::Ordering;

/// Return a 0-based rank array for each element in `lst0`.
/// Equal values get the same rank (the minimal sorted position among them).
/// Only one sort of indices is performed.
pub fn ranks_min_for_ties<T>(lst0: &Array1<T>) -> Array1<usize>
where
    T: PartialOrd + PartialEq + Copy,
{
    let n = lst0.len();
    // trivial case
    if n == 0 {
        return Array1::from(vec![]);
    }

    // Permutation of indices 0..n
    let mut perm: Vec<usize> = (0..n).collect();

    // Sort indices by the corresponding values in lst0 (one sort only)
    perm.sort_by(|&i, &j| {
        match lst0[i].partial_cmp(&lst0[j]) {
            Some(ord) => ord,
            // Treat incomparable (e.g. NaN) as equal to preserve stability
            None => Ordering::Equal,
        }
    });

    // Build rank array: for each position in sorted order, assign a rank.
    // If the value equals the previous value, reuse the previous rank (ties get same rank).
    let mut ranks_vec = vec![0usize; n];
    let mut last_rank = 0usize;
    ranks_vec[perm[0]] = last_rank;

    for pos in 1..n {
        let idx = perm[pos];
        let prev_idx = perm[pos - 1];
        if lst0[idx] != lst0[prev_idx] {
            // new (larger) value gets rank = current position (pos)
            last_rank = pos;
        }
        ranks_vec[idx] = last_rank;
    }

    Array1::from(ranks_vec)
}

fn main() {
    // Example usage
    let a = Array1::from(vec![0.2, 0.5, 0.5, 0.1]);
    let r = ranks_min_for_ties(&a);
    println!("values: {:?}", a);
    println!("ranks (0-based, ties -> same rank): {:?}", r);
    // For above example you should see ranks: [1, 2, 2, 0]
}
```

If you prefer 1-based ranks, change `last_rank = pos;` to `last_rank = pos + 1;` and initialize the first rank accordingly. If you want "dense" ranks (no gaps after ties), replace `last_rank = pos;` with `last_rank += 1` and initialize the first rank to 1 (or 0 for 0-based dense ranks).

