## Alternative Version with Tie-Breaking (Dense Ranking)

If you want each unique value to have a unique rank (dense ranking):

```rust
use ndarray::Array1;
use std::cmp::Ordering;

fn assign_ranks_dense(lst0: &Array1<f64>) -> Array1<usize> {
    let n = lst0.len();

    let mut indexed: Vec<(usize, f64)> = lst0
        .iter()
        .enumerate()
        .map(|(i, &val)| (i, val))
        .collect();

    // Single sort
    indexed.sort_by(|a, b| {
        a.1.partial_cmp(&b.1)
            .unwrap_or(Ordering::Equal)
    });

    let mut ranks = Array1::zeros(n);
    let mut current_rank = 1;
    let mut last_value = indexed[0].1;

    for (rank_idx, (original_idx, value)) in indexed.iter().enumerate() {
        if rank_idx > 0 && (value - last_value).abs() > f64::EPSILON {
            current_rank = rank_idx + 1;
        }
        ranks[*original_idx] = current_rank;
        last_value = *value;
    }

    ranks
}
```

## Key Features

1. **Single Sort**: The function sorts only once by creating an indexed vector and sorting it
2. **Original Order Preservation**: Uses indices to track original positions
3. **Efficient**: O(n log n) time complexity due to sorting
4. **Generic Ready**: Easy to modify for other types implementing `PartialOrd`
5. **1-Indexed Ranks**: Smallest value gets rank 1

