### Converting a Labeled Tabular Dataset to a List of Dictionaries

Below is a self-contained Rust function that uses the `serde_json` library to convert a labeled tabular dataset into a list of dictionaries. This function assumes that the input dataset is represented as a `serde_json::Value` where each key is a column name and each value is an array of column values.

```rust
use serde_json::Value;
use serde_json::Map;

/// Convert a labeled tabular dataset into a list of dictionaries.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `df` - The input dataset as a `serde_json::Value`.
///
/// # Returns
///
/// A `Vec` of `Map`s, where each `Map` represents a row in the dataset.
pub fn convert_to_dicts(df: Value) -> Vec<Map<String, Value>> {
    // Check if the input is an object
    if let Value::Object(obj) = df {
        // Get the column names and values
        let columns: Vec<String> = obj.keys().cloned().collect();
        let values: Vec<Vec<Value>> = obj.values().map(|v| {
            if let Value::Array(arr) = v {
                arr.clone()
            } else {
                panic!("Invalid input: expected an array of values");
            }
        }).collect();

        // Check if all columns have the same number of values
        let row_count = values[0].len();
        if !values.iter().all(|v| v.len() == row_count) {
            panic!("Invalid input: all columns must have the same number of values");
        }

        // Create a list of dictionaries
        let mut result = Vec::new();
        for i in 0..row_count {
            let mut row = Map::new();
            row.insert("index".to_string(), Value::Number(serde_json::Number::from(i)));
            for (j, column) in columns.iter().enumerate() {
                row.insert(column.clone(), values[j][i].clone());
            }
            result.push(row);
        }

        result
    } else {
        panic!("Invalid input: expected an object");
    }
}

fn main() {
    // Example usage
    let df = serde_json::json!({
        "A": [1, 2, 3],
        "B": [4, 5, 6],
        "C": [7, 8, 9]
    });

    let dicts = convert_to_dicts(df);
    println!("{:?}", dicts);
}
```

This function works by first checking if the input is an object. If it is, it extracts the column names and values from the object. It then checks if all columns have the same number of values. If they do, it creates a list of dictionaries, where each dictionary represents a row in the dataset. The dictionary includes the row's index alongside its column-value pairs. Finally, it returns the list of dictionaries.

The example usage in the `main` function demonstrates how to use this function to convert a labeled tabular dataset into a list of dictionaries. The output will be a `Vec` of `Map`s, where each `Map` represents a row in the dataset.

