Notes:
- This example uses an RBTree keyed by an i32. You can adapt the Item type and KeyAdapter to your own key-type and stored payload.
- The CursorMut::remove() call removes the current node and moves the cursor to the next element; this lets us continue scanning without skipping or revisiting nodes.

Code:

```rust
use intrusive_collections::{intrusive_adapter, KeyAdapter, RBTree, RBTreeLink};
use std::cmp::Ordering;

// A simple item stored in the intrusive RBTree.
struct Item {
    link: RBTreeLink, // required link for intrusive RBTree
    key: i32,
    payload: String,
}

// Create an adapter that allows the RBTree to access the link inside Box<Item>.
intrusive_adapter!(ItemAdapter = Box<Item>: Item { link: RBTreeLink });

// Implement KeyAdapter so RBTree can extract a key to order items.
impl<'a> KeyAdapter<'a> for ItemAdapter {
    type Key = i32;
    fn get_key(&self, entry: &'a Item) -> &'a i32 {
        &entry.key
    }
}

/// Remove all elements matching `pred` from `tree`.
///
/// This walks the tree once with a mutable cursor and calls `cursor.remove()` for each matching
/// element. This is efficient when the number of removals is small: each removal is O(log n)
/// (rebalancing), and we do a single in-order scan.
fn remove_if<F>(tree: &mut RBTree<ItemAdapter>, mut pred: F)
where
    F: FnMut(&Item) -> bool,
{
    // Start a mutable cursor at the front (smallest key).
    let mut cur = tree.front_mut();
    while let Some(node_ref) = cur.get() {
        if pred(node_ref) {
            // Remove the current node. Cursor moves to the next element.
            cur.remove();
        } else {
            // Advance to next element when not removing.
            cur.move_next();
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    // Comparator for keys (i32 implements Ord; we still pass an explicit comparator).
    let cmp = |a: &i32, b: &i32| a.cmp(b);

    // Create the tree.
    let mut tree = RBTree::new(ItemAdapter::new(), cmp);

    // Insert some items.
    for i in 0..20 {
        let item = Box::new(Item {
            link: RBTreeLink::new(),
            key: i,
            payload: format!("value-{}", i),
        });
        tree.insert(item);
    }

    // Remove items whose key is a multiple of 7 (a small number of removals).
    remove_if(&mut tree, |it| it.key % 7 == 0);

    // Print remaining keys to verify removals.
    let mut cursor = tree.front();
    let mut remaining = Vec::new();
    while let Some(item_ref) = cursor.get() {
        remaining.push(item_ref.key);
        cursor.move_next();
    }
    println!("Remaining keys: {:?}", remaining);
}
```

Why this is efficient for a small number of removals:
- We do a single in-order scan (O(n)) but the cost per non-removed element is tiny (just pointer traversals). If the tree is large and you truly want to avoid an O(n) walk when the number of removals is tiny, you should supply either (a) a predicate that can be translated to a small set of key lookups (and then locate each matching key with O(log n) searches), or (b) an iterator/collection of candidate keys to remove. The approach above is simple and safe when an O(n) scan is acceptable cost-wise; it minimizes allocations and performs the tree rebalancing only for the actual removals.

